# Backend (`apps/backend`)

NestJS API. Stay inside `apps/backend` unless the task needs Prisma or infra.

## Stack

- NestJS 11, TypeScript, Jest, ESLint, Prettier, **tsx** (dev and Compose runtime — not `nest start` / webpack)
- Entry: `src/main.ts`, root module: `src/app.module.ts`
- Runtime in Compose: `tsx watch`. Use `@Inject(...)` for constructor DI under tsx.
- Prisma: `PrismaService` extends generated client with **`Pool` + `@prisma/adapter-pg`**; disconnect with `$disconnect()` and `pool.end()`.
- Prisma access via `__prisma/*` path alias only — **never** add `apps/prisma` as an npm/workspace package dependency of backend.
- Client import: `__prisma/generated/prisma/client`. Public shapes: `__prisma/types/...` (e.g. `PublicUser`).
- Jest: `moduleNameMapper` for `^__prisma/(.*)$` → `<rootDir>/../prisma/$1` (unit and e2e configs).

## Scaffolding (host only)

- Create Nest entities **only** with Nest CLI on the **host** (never inside a container), e.g. `npx nest g module|controller|provider|service … --no-spec`.
- Reason: avoid root-owned files and `permission denied` / git chown issues from container writes.
- After generate, fill in logic in the created files — do not hand-create parallel `*.module.ts` / `*.controller.ts` / `*.provider.ts` trees.

## Conventions

- Domain modules under `src/<domain>/` (e.g. `auth/`, `redis/`, `users/`).
- One concrete provider/service class — **no** separate abstract interface + `*Impl` pair unless explicitly requested.
- Prefer Nest names from CLI (`*.provider.ts`, `*.service.ts`, `*.controller.ts`, `*.module.ts`).
- DTOs and zod validation next to the controller (e.g. `auth/dto.ts`) or in `dto/`.
- Business logic in providers/services, not controllers.
- Do not return secrets (e.g. `passwordHash`) from API providers — use `PublicUser` / Prisma Client `omit` / explicit `select`.
- Tests: `*.spec.ts` next to code; e2e in `test/`.
- Do not edit generated Prisma Client here — schema lives in `apps/prisma`.
- Frontend may `import type` of Nest controllers from `__backend/...` and derive HTTP response types as `Awaited<ReturnType<SomeController['method']>>`. Declare explicit return types on controller methods so those contracts stay stable.
- Prefer `import type` for type-only Nest imports (`CanActivate`, `OnModuleInit`, …) so frontend typecheck (`verbatimModuleSyntax`) stays clean when pulling controllers.

## Auth / Redis

- Sessions: opaque `access_token` / `refresh_token` cookies (httpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, Domain from `COOKIE_DOMAIN`); Redis holds SHA-256 hashes — details in `redis` rule.
- Module: `src/auth/` — login / logout / refresh / check / me; `GET /auth/check` returns `0|1` by access-session presence (no required guard); lazy `resolveSessionUser` middleware; `AuthGuard` (`strict` default true; `new AuthGuard({ strict: false })` allows anonymous); `@SessionUser()`.
- Redis client: `src/redis/` (`ioredis`) — see `redis` rule.
- Cookie names/paths/TTL are constants in `auth/auth.constants.ts` (not env), except Domain.

## Commands (from `apps/backend` on the host)

```bash
npx nest g module <name> --no-spec
npx nest g controller <name> --no-spec
npx nest g provider <name> --no-spec
npm run start:dev
npm run lint
npm run test
```

